{
  "raw": {
    "https://fa-etnb-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/4184": {
      "title": "Senior Faculty Technician",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/11/2026 Category: Technical and Specialist PrimaryLocation: Newcastle City Campus, United Kingdom As a Senior Technician, you will provide specialist technical expertise to support research, teaching, and commercial activities across the School of Engineering, Physics and Mathematics. ABOUT THE ROLE As a Senior Technician, you will provide specialist technical expertise to support research, teaching, and commercial activities across the School of Engineering, Physics and Mathematics. The role will be instrumental in the management, maintenance, and enhancement of the North East Space Skills and Technology (NESST) facilities (not exclusively), working in partnership with academic staff, industry collaborators, and space sector specialists to advance research, technology development, and workforce skills. As part of a multidisciplinary technical team, working alongside other technicians and the Scientific Officer, you will support pioneering activity in areas including optical satellite communications, satellite manufacturing and testing, space weather and space-based energy. You will play a key role in enabling world-class research, delivering specialist technical training, and helping to establish NESST as a centre of excellence for space skills, innovation and education. Further information is available in the job description . ABOUT THE TEAM Within the School of Engineering, Physics and Mathematics at the Faculty of Science and Environment, the Department of Physics focuses on a broad spectrum of research areas, with core groups in Astrophysics (including Solar and Space Physics), Space Technology, Photovoltaics, Renewable Energy Technology and Materials, and Smart Materials & Surface We take a highly collaborative approach, working with a diverse range of academic and industry partners. The successful candidate will be based in the NESST Building on our City Campus, as part of a multidisciplinary technical team comprising one Scientific Officer and a Laboratory Technician, all led by a Technical Manager. ABOUT YOU To be successful in the role you will have a degree or HND, preferably in Aerospace Engineering, Physics, Material Science, Engineering or a related technical discipline. Experience in operating, maintaining and laboratory or cleanroom equipment, particularly environmental testing and vacuum-based systems. The role also requires a proven ability to independently diagnose complex technical faults in equipment operation and to implement effective solutions. Experience in a technical role within cleanroom facilities is desirable. You will possess excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to guide, train and support a diverse range of users, from students and early-career researchers to experienced academics and external partners. You will be equally comfortable working independently and contributing as part of a collaborative technical team. Strong organisational and time-management skills are essential, together with the ability to manage competing priorities, coordinate multiple activities simultaneously and consistently deliver high-quality outcomes within agreed timescales. The role requires a proactive approach to responding to changing operational demands while maintaining a high standard of service delivery. A high level of accuracy and attention to detail is required when maintaining technical records and documentation, ensuring compliance with relevant policies, procedures, and quality standards. This includes the management of equipment maintenance records, data logs, operational documentation, incident reports, and other compliance-related information. Knowledge and proven experience in the implementation and management of health and safety requirements within science and engineering environments are required. You will be confident in developing, reviewing and applying risk assessments, standard operating procedures, COSHH assessments, and other compliance documentation, while providing guidance to staff and students to ensure safe and effective working practices. Further information is available in the person specification . If you would like an informal discussion about the role, please contact Demelza Menendez (demelza.menendez@northumbria.ac.uk) To apply for this vacancy please click 'Apply Now'. Your application should include a covering letter and a CV. ABOUT US Join Northumbria University, a research-intensive institution unlocking potential and changing lives locally and globally. Named Times Higher Education's University of the Year in 2022 and Modern University of the Year in 2025, we rank top 25 in the UK for research power. Discover more about us . With over 37,000 students from 140+ countries, we offer world-leading research, award-winning partnerships, and an outstanding student experience. We empower our exceptional staff, promoting a positive work-life balance and offering great benefits, including excellent pension schemes, flexible working, generous holiday entitlement and more . Our Northumbria Values, co-created by our team, define who we are: Academic Excellence, Innovation, Inclusivity, Collaboration, and Ambition. Our Behaviours shape our work culture: We listen and learn, support one another, respect everyone, trust each other, and are bold. Based in Newcastle upon Tyne and London, we are an on-campus organisation and offer flexible hours and location where the role allows. We pride ourselves on diversity and inclusivity, holding numerous awards for gender and race equality, disability confidence, and research excellence. We also hold the HR Excellence in Research award for implementing the concordat supporting the career Development of Researchers and are members of the Euraxess initiative to deliver information and support to professional researchers. The University has implemented a range of flexible working arrangements, and we are happy to explore candidate requirements as part of the recruitment process. Apply now and join a community committed to transforming society and the economy. Explore \" Our Exceptional People , Our Values and Behaviours \" page to learn more about our commitments and see our Privacy Policy for further details. "
    },
    "https://fa-etnb-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/4280": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Category: Teaching and Research PrimaryLocation: Newcastle City Campus, United Kingdom We are excited to offer 3 post-doctoral research associate positions that are each funded for five years commencing in April 2027. ABOUT THE PROJECT AND ROLES We are excited to offer 3 post-doctoral research associate positions PDRA1, PDRA2 and PDRA3 that are each funded for five years commencing in April 2027. PLEASE INDICATE IN YOUR APPLICATION WHICH POST/POSTS YOU ARE INTERESTED IN Why is the response of Earth’s outer radiation belt to solar wind driving so hard to predict? is a five-year multi-institution STFC-funded project . Our aim is to determine why the terrestrial outer radiation belt environment is currently difficult to predict. Improving this predictive capability matters well beyond our field: space weather events driven by solar activity can damage or disable satellites, disrupt GPS and communications systems, and pose radiation risks to astronauts and high-altitude flights. Better models of the outer radiation belt will directly support satellite operators, space mission planners, and national space weather forecasting services such as the UK Met Office Space Weather Operations Centre. We plan six workstreams to be undertaken as part of the project, three of which will be conducted at Northumbria University: PDRA1 will work with Prof Jonathan Rae to conduct investigations into the connections between solar wind driving and the energy stored in Earth’s plasma sheet PDRA2 will work with Prof Clare Watt to conduct investigations into the connection between Earth’s plasma sheet and the inner magnetosphere PDRA3 will work with Dr Sarah Bentley to conduct numerical experiments on physical processes that act in the outer radiation belts. Although each workstream will be conducted by different researchers in the team, success of the project will depend upon close collaboration between researchers. You will therefore lead the work on your own workstream in addition to collaborating with the wider consortium. You will be encouraged to contribute your own ideas to further the objectives of the project. We will support you to attend regular consortium meetings virtually and in-person, as far as your personal circumstances allow. Travel to conferences and workshops to present results and engage with the international research community is supported by the project and will be encouraged where practicable. For an informal discussion about any of these posts please contact Professor Clare Watt at clare.watt@northumbria.ac.uk. For further information about other positions in this project, please see the vacancy pages at Warwick University and the University of Birmingham. ABOUT THE TEAM The Project Lead is Professor Clare Watt of the School of Engineering, Physics and Mathematics in Northumbria University, with co-Leads Dr Sarah Bentley and Prof Jonathan Rae at Northumbria, together with Dr Ravindra Desai at Warwick University and Dr Oliver Allanson at the University of Birmingham. The full consortium team will comprise the researchers above, the advertised three PDRAs and a Research Software Engineer (RSE) at Northumbria University, a PDRA and RSE at Warwick University and a PDRA and RSE at University of Birmingham. We expect a number of PhD students to be added to the team as the project progresses. At Northumbria University, your work will be undertaken in the Solar and Space Physics research group. Our group researches the fundamental plasma physics of the solar system, including the Sun, the solar wind, and the plasma environments of Earth, other planets and comets. We support a thriving PhD and postdoctoral community with multiple opportunities to develop new skills and expertise. The project will be undertaken in the North East Space Skills and Technology Centre (NESST) . This £50million state-of-the-art building is a UK-wide space asset, training the next generation of space-related engineers and housing multiple space-related research projects . ABOUT YOU We seek applicants with a background in space plasma physics and/or related field to the project that you are applying to, with demonstrable specialist expert knowledge in magnetospheric physics. You will have expertise in data analysis (especially PDRA1 and PDRA2) and/or numerical modelling (especially PDRA3). In addition to the expertise you bring to the role, you will be encouraged to explore new methods beyond your current experience and develop your expertise in new techniques in space physics research. We expect applicants to be strongly motivated to work with other researchers on a consortium project. We recognise that excellent researchers come from a wide range of backgrounds, and we are committed to building a team that reflects the diversity of our field and beyond. We warmly welcome applications from researchers who are under-represented in space physics, including those from minority ethnic backgrounds, disabled researchers, and those returning from career breaks. We understand that career paths are rarely linear, and we value the breadth of experience and perspective that different backgrounds bring to research. If you would like to discuss flexible working arrangements, reasonable adjustments, or any other aspect of the role before applying, please don’t hesitate to get in touch with Professor Clare Watt at clare.watt@northumbria.ac.uk. Please see role and person specification for more details of the skills, knowledge and experience required and desired for these posts: PDRA 1 PDRA 2 PDRA 3 To apply for this vacancy please click 'Apply Now' . Your application should include a covering letter and a CV. We welcome applications from the UK and across the world. Visit our web pages for details about Relocation Assistance . ABOUT US Join Northumbria University, a research-intensive institution unlocking potential and changing lives locally and globally. Named Times Higher Education's University of the Year in 2022 and Modern University of the Year in 2025, we rank top 25 in the UK for research power. Discover more about us . With over 37,000 students from 140+ countries, we offer world-leading research, award-winning partnerships, and an outstanding student experience. We empower our exceptional staff, promoting a positive work-life balance and offering great benefits, including excellent pension schemes, flexible working, generous holiday entitlement and more . Our Northumbria Values, co-created by our team, define who we are: Academic Excellence, Innovation, Inclusivity, Collaboration, and Ambition. Our Behaviours shape our work culture: We listen and learn, support one another, respect everyone, trust each other, and are bold. Based in Newcastle upon Tyne and London, we are an on-campus organisation and offer flexible hours and location where the role allows. We pride ourselves on diversity and inclusivity, holding a Silver Athena Swan in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality, a bronze Race Equality Charter Award and we are a Disability Confident Leader. We also hold the HR Excellence in Research award for implementing the concordat supporting the career Development of Researchers and are members of the Euraxess initiative to deliver information and support to professional researchers. The University has implemented a range of flexible working arrangements, and we are happy to explore candidate requirements as part of the recruitment process. Apply now and join a community committed to transforming society and the economy. Explore \" Our Exceptional People , Our Values and Behaviours \" page to learn more about our commitments and see our Privacy Policy for further details. "
    },
    "https://fa-etnb-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/4284": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Category: Teaching and Research PrimaryLocation: Newcastle City Campus, United Kingdom We are excited to offer a post-doctoral research associate position that is funded for one year commencing in April 2027. ABOUT THE PROJECT AND ROLE We are excited to offer a post-doctoral research associate position that is funded for one year commencing in April 2027. How can we accurately forecast geomagnetic storms days in advance? Geomagnetic storms are large disturbances in near-Earth space, driven by the Sun. These storms can have severe consequences for critical modern infrastructure, such as power networks, satellites and communications. Infrastructure operators need sufficient warning time to take mitigating actions, such as cancelling scheduled maintenance, bringing spare capacity online, or protecting vulnerable assets. The aim of this project is to build on existing promising initial approaches, coupling computationally efficient numerical models of the solar wind to machine-learning frameworks to produce accurate, useful forecasts at long lead times. While this post is based at Northumbria University, the wider project (Solar Tsunamis) involves an international consortium, led by Prof. Craig Rodger at the University of Otago, New Zealand. There will be the opportunity to attend consortium meetings with other collaborators, as well as travel to conferences and workshops to engage with the international research community. For an informal discussion about the post, please contact Dr Andy Smith at andy.w.smith@northumbria.ac.uk. ABOUT THE TEAM The Project Lead is Dr Andy Smith of the School of Engineering, Physics and Mathematics in Northumbria University. The Solar Tsunamis project is an international consortia led by Prof. Craig Rodger at the University of Otago, New Zealand. At Northumbria University, your work will be undertaken in the Solar and Space Physics research group. Our group researches the fundamental plasma physics of the solar system, including the Sun, the solar wind, and the plasma environments of Earth, other planets and comets. We support a thriving PhD and postdoctoral community with multiple opportunities to develop new skills and expertise. The project will be undertaken in the North East Space Skills and Technology Centre (NESST) . This £50million state-of-the-art building is a UK-wide space asset, training the next generation of space-related engineers and housing multiple space-related research projects . ABOUT YOU We seek applicants with a background in space plasma physics and machine learning. You will have expertise in data analysis and with numerical modelling. In addition to the expertise you bring to the role, you will be encouraged to explore new methods beyond your current experience and develop your expertise in new techniques in space physics research. We expect applicants to be strongly motivated to work with other researchers on a consortium project. We recognise that excellent researchers come from a wide range of backgrounds, and we are committed to building a team that reflects the diversity of our field and beyond. We warmly welcome applications from researchers who are under-represented in space physics, including those from minority ethnic backgrounds, disabled researchers, and those returning from career breaks. We understand that career paths are rarely linear, and we value the breadth of experience and perspective that different backgrounds bring to research. If you would like to discuss flexible working arrangements, reasonable adjustments, or any other aspect of the role before applying, please don’t hesitate to get in touch with Dr Andy Smith at andy.w.smith@northumbria.ac.uk. Please see the role description and person specification for more details of the skills, knowledge and experience required and desired for this post. ABOUT US Join Northumbria University, a research-intensive institution unlocking potential and changing lives locally and globally. Named Times Higher Education's University of the Year in 2022 and Modern University of the Year in 2025, we rank top 25 in the UK for research power. Discover more about us . With over 37,000 students from 140+ countries, we offer world-leading research, award-winning partnerships, and an outstanding student experience. We empower our exceptional staff, promoting a positive work-life balance and offering great benefits, including excellent pension schemes, flexible working, generous holiday entitlement and more . Our Northumbria Values, co-created by our team, define who we are: Academic Excellence, Innovation, Inclusivity, Collaboration, and Ambition. Our Behaviours shape our work culture: We listen and learn, support one another, respect everyone, trust each other, and are bold. Based in Newcastle upon Tyne and London, we are an on-campus organisation and offer flexible hours and location where the role allows. We pride ourselves on diversity and inclusivity, holding a Silver Athena Swan in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality, a bronze Race Equality Charter Award and we are a Disability Confident Leader. We also hold the HR Excellence in Research award for implementing the concordat supporting the career Development of Researchers and are members of the Euraxess initiative to deliver information and support to professional researchers. The University has implemented a range of flexible working arrangements, and we are happy to explore candidate requirements as part of the recruitment process. Apply now and join a community committed to transforming society and the economy. Explore \" Our Exceptional People , Our Values and Behaviours \" page to learn more about our commitments and see our Privacy Policy for further details. "
    },
    "https://fa-etnb-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/4242": {
      "title": "Associate Professor in Design",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Category: Teaching and Research PrimaryLocation: Newcastle City Campus, United Kingdom Are you an exceptional academic with a growing international profile in Interior Design area seeking an opportunity to lead academic teams, using your research to inform the delivery of exceptional teaching and support student success? ABOUT THE ROLE We are seeking an Associate Professor in interior design at Northumbria University's School of Design, Arts and Creative Industries. The post-holder will deliver high-quality studio-based teaching at undergraduate and postgraduate level and raise the profile and performance of our research and practice across the wider design sector. We welcome applications from candidates with a track-record of research, scholarship, or practice-based enquiry in interior design, interior architecture, or a closely related field. You will bring an innovative approach to teaching and research, and place environmental and societal challenges at the centre of your work. Interior design at Northumbria spans the commercial design of interior spaces, from retail environments to large-scale public visitor attractions. This is a senior academic appointment with scope to influence the direction of the subject, mentor colleagues, and build partnerships with industry and cultural organisations. Further information is available in the Associate Professor job description. ABOUT THE TEAM The School of Design, Arts and Creative Industries (DACI) at Northumbria University is a leading provider of graduates to UK design and creative industries and has an outstanding track-record for design leadership amongst its alumni. Our graduates go on to shape practice across commercial, hospitality, healthcare, residential and heritage sectors. The school prioritises societal transformation and green-transition across all design disciplines, which include product, industrial and service design areas. DACI is a large school with approximately 1,600 students with 120 staff and offers programmes from foundation level to PhD in design and creative subjects. The school prioritises inclusion and access, aiming to break down barriers so that all students can thrive within the creative industries. More information can be found here ABOUT YOU As an externally recognised academic with a strong national and or international reputation you will evidence sustained research output of a quality that is internationally excellent in terms of originality, significance, and rigour. An experienced academic leader you will demonstrate success in the leadership and management of people, teams, and projects, in particular supporting the development of academic colleagues. You should demonstrate definition and development of your academic field and have significant and strong record of securing funding from a range of sources. Further information is available in the Associate Professor person specification. If you would like an informal discussion about the role, please contact the Head of School Professor Ambrose Field on daci.fa@northumbria.ac.uk We welcome applications from the UK and across the world. Visit our web pages for details about Relocation Assistance . This opportunity is open to applications from internal candidates who do not hold a current substantive academic role at Northumbria. Current academic colleagues with a substantive role are encouraged to consider options to progress via the Academic Development Review process. Pay, Pensions and Total Reward Approach Northumbria University offers a competitive reward package for academic colleagues through our Total Reward Approach. This combines salary and pension provision to provide flexibility and choice, enabling colleagues to select the option that best suits their individual circumstances. Academic colleagues can choose to join either the Teachers' Pension Scheme (TPS) or the Universities Superannuation Scheme (USS). Further information about our Total Reward Approach, including pay, pension options and career progression, can be found on our dedicated webpage . To apply for this vacancy please click 'Apply Now'. Your application should include a covering letter and a CV. Please ensure your CV includes research / education statements, and grants and publications list highlighting your highest quality research outputs up to a maximum of four. For your most recent grants please include the grant title, funder reference, funder, amount, and your role. In addition, you are asked to provide copies of the top two research outputs (Journal / book chapter / practice-based portfolio). ABOUT US Join Northumbria University, a research-intensive institution unlocking potential and changing lives locally and globally. Named Times Higher Education's University of the Year in 2022 and Modern University of the Year in 2025, we rank top 25 in the UK for research power. Discover more about us . With over 37,000 students from 140+ countries, we offer world-leading research, award-winning partnerships, and an outstanding student experience. We empower our exceptional staff, promoting a positive work-life balance and offering great benefits, including excellent pension schemes, flexible working, generous holiday entitlement and more . Our Northumbria Values, co-created by our team, define who we are: Academic Excellence, Innovation, Inclusivity, Collaboration, and Ambition. Our Behaviours shape our work culture: We listen and learn, support one another, respect everyone, trust each other, and are bold. Based in Newcastle upon Tyne and London, we are an on-campus organisation and offer flexible hours and location where the role allows. We pride ourselves on diversity and inclusivity, holding a Silver Athena Swan in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality, a bronze Race Equality Charter Award and we are a Disability Confident Leader. We also hold the HR Excellence in Research award for implementing the concordat supporting the career Development of Researchers and are members of the Euraxess initiative to deliver information and support to professional researchers. The University has implemented a range of flexible working arrangements, and we are happy to explore candidate requirements as part of the recruitment process. Apply now and join a community committed to transforming society and the economy. Explore \" Our Exceptional People , Our Values and Behaviours \" page to learn more about our commitments and see our Privacy Policy for further details. "
    },
    "https://fa-etnb-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/4295": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Psychology",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/11/2026 Category: Teaching and Research PrimaryLocation: Newcastle City Campus, United Kingdom Are you an experienced Academic/Practitioner looking for a role that lets you apply your research or professional expertise in contemporary practice within our programmes to deliver exceptional teaching and support student success? ABOUT THE ROLES We are seeking to appoint three Assistant Professors in the School of Psychology: Assistant Professor in Occupational and Organisational Psychology (1.0 FTE - 37 hrs per week) Assistant Professor in Psychology (up to 0.8 FTE - 29.6 hrs per week) Assistant Professor in Forensic Psychology (1.0 FTE – 37 hours per week) Working as part of multidisciplinary teams, you will help the Head of School and Associate Heads of School maintain and enhance the national and international reputation of the School of Psychology and the Faculty of Health and Wellbeing. You will demonstrate continued academic and/or practitioner achievement while contributing to your discipline and to the life and work of the Faculty. You will take responsibility for module and/or programme leadership, and will deliver high-quality teaching across the School's undergraduate and postgraduate portfolios. Assistant Professor in Occupational and Organisational Psychology (1.0 FTE - 37 hrs per week) Do you have expertise in occupational psychology as a practitioner and/or academic? Are you looking for a role in an academic environment, teaching the next generation of occupational psychologists and conducting research or practice in the field? You will draw on your proven track record as an occupational psychologist in a practitioner and/or academic context. You will be part of a team of psychologists and occupational and organisational psychologists who support delivery of our Occupational Psychology provision, which includes undergraduate pathway options, master's provision and a professional doctorate in occupational psychology. You will teach on topics such as contemporary issues in occupational psychology, occupational psychology theory, and elements of occupational psychology practice, and will hold module and/or programme leadership responsibilities. We are seeking either an Academic Occupational Psychologist or a Practitioner Occupational Psychologist. We are particularly interested in hearing from you if you have experience and knowledge in organisational culture, structure, design and change. Assistant Professor in Psychology (up to 0.8 FTE - 29.6 hrs per week) We are seeking an Assistant Professor in any area of Psychology to build and sustain a nationally and internationally recognised research and/or practice profile. You will draw on your proven track record to deliver high-quality teaching across the School's undergraduate and postgraduate portfolios and will take responsibility for module and/or programme leadership. Assistant Professor in Forensic Psychology (1.0 FTE – 37 hours per week) You will draw on your proven track record as a forensic psychologist in a practitioner and/ or academic context. You will function as part of a team of psychologists and forensic psychologists, who will support the delivery of a new MSc Forensic Psychology programme within the School of Psychology. You will deliver teaching on a range of topics such as contemporary issues in forensic psychology, forensic psychology theory, and elements of forensic psychology practice. You will have responsibilities relating to module leadership and/ or programme leadership. We are seeking either an Academic Forensic Psychologist or Practitioner Forensic Psychologist. These posts are advertised across our Assistant Professor and Assistant Professor (Practice) roles. Full time and part time applications welcome. ABOUT THE TEAM The School of Psychology sits within the newly named Faculty of Health and Wellbeing and plays a pivotal role in the development of Northumbria University. Other Schools within this Faculty include Healthcare and Nursing Sciences, Communities & Education, and Sport, Exercise and Rehabilitation, all creating a dynamic and purposeful environment for education, research and enterprise across health and society. Northumbria is a research-intensive, modern university with a clear social mission: to unlock potential for all, changing lives regionally, nationally, and internationally. Our vision is that through world-leading research and transformative education, we enable people from all backgrounds to succeed, and support businesses and communities across the North-East, London, and beyond to thrive. The School of Psychology boasts outstanding research, a strong and diverse student community, and highly engaged academic and professional staff. We have a well-respected portfolio of BPS-accredited programmes, including MSc programmes in Health Psychology, Occupational Psychology, Sport and Exercise Psychology, and MSc Psychology; our most recent addition is Forensic Psychology. We also offer undergraduate programmes in Psychology and Psychology with Criminology and deliver both on campus and via distance learning. ABOUT YOU To be successful in the role you will have demonstrable experience and knowledge in your specified discipline, and you must have experience in and/or a desire to help shape education within the school. A recognised teaching qualification or equivalent teaching experience (such as Fellowship of AdvanceHE) will be beneficial to your application. Applicants for the Assistant Professor (Practice) role should hold a first degree in a relevant subject and professional qualification or substantial professional experience in the discipline. You must also be able to demonstrate contemporary expert specialist knowledge and subject expertise within the relevant discipline/practice areas ensuring that the curriculum reflects the latest developments within practice. Experience of designing, delivering, assessing, and evaluating high-quality teaching and learning practice as well as evidence of contributions to the development of contemporary practice are essential. Further information about the requirements of the Assistant Professor and Assistant Professor (Practice) roles is available in the person specifications. If you would like an informal discussion about the Occupational and Organisational Psychology role, please contact Professor Anthony Montgomery . Please contact Dr Kris McCarty, Head of Education in the School of Psychology, or Dr Jo Greer , Head of Subject for Postgraduate Psychology programmes, to discuss the other posts. We welcome applications from the UK and across the world. Visit our web pages for details about Relocation Assistance . This opportunity is open to applications from internal candidates who do not hold a current substantive academic role at Northumbria. Current academic colleagues with a substantive role are encouraged to consider options to progress via the Academic Development Review process. Pay, Pensions and Total Reward Approach Northumbria University offers a competitive reward package for academic colleagues through our Total Reward Approach. This combines salary and pension provision to provide flexibility and choice, enabling colleagues to select the option that best suits their individual circumstances. Academic colleagues can choose to join either the Teachers' Pension Scheme (TPS) or the Universities Superannuation Scheme (USS). Further information about our Total Reward Approach, including pay, pension options and career progression, can be found on our dedicated webpage . To apply for this vacancy please click 'Apply Now'. Your application should include a covering letter and a CV. Please ensure your CV includes research / education statements, and grants and publications list highlighting your highest quality research outputs up to a maximum of four. For your most recent grants please include the grant title, funder reference, funder, amount, and your role. In addition, you are asked to provide copies of the top two research outputs (Journal / book chapter / practice-based portfolio). The closing date for applications is 2nd November at 23:59. ABOUT US Join Northumbria University, a research-intensive institution unlocking potential and changing lives locally and globally. Named Times Higher Education's University of the Year in 2022 and Modern University of the Year in 2025, we rank top 25 in the UK for research power. Discover more about us . With over 37,000 students from 140+ countries, we offer world-leading research, award-winning partnerships, and an outstanding student experience. We empower our exceptional staff, promoting a positive work-life balance and offering great benefits, including excellent pension schemes, flexible working, generous holiday entitlement and more . Our Northumbria Values, co-created by our team, define who we are: Academic Excellence, Innovation, Inclusivity, Collaboration, and Ambition. Our Behaviours shape our work culture: We listen and learn, support one another, respect everyone, trust each other, and are bold. Based in Newcastle upon Tyne and London, we are an on-campus organisation and offer flexible hours and location where the role allows. We pride ourselves on diversity and inclusivity, holding a Silver Athena Swan in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality, a bronze Race Equality Charter Award and we are a Disability Confident Leader. We also hold the HR Excellence in Research award for implementing the concordat supporting the career Development of Researchers and are members of the Euraxess initiative to deliver information and support to professional researchers. The University has implemented a range of flexible working arrangements, and we are happy to explore candidate requirements as part of the recruitment process. Apply now and join a community committed to transforming society and the economy. Explore \" Our Exceptional People , Our Values and Behaviours \" page to learn more about our commitments and see our Privacy Policy for further details. "
    },
    "https://fa-etnb-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/4205": {
      "title": "Campus Support Officer Nights",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Category: Operational PrimaryLocation: Newcastle City Campus, United Kingdom We are seeking highly motivated, professional and customer-focused individuals to join our Security Team as a Campus Support Officer (Nights). You will play a key role in maintaining a safe, secure and welcoming environment for students, staff and visitors across the University campuses. ABOUT THE ROLE The University Security Team plays a vital role in maintaining a safe, secure and welcoming environment for students, staff and visitors. The team provides a wide range of services including crime prevention, patrols, emergency response, building security, fire response, traffic and car parking management, event security and support to students living in University accommodation. The role is based primarily at the City and Coach Lane Campuses , with staff also providing security and emergency response cover across other University locations, including Halls of Residence and other University premises as required. The role operates on a night-shift pattern , including weekends, bank holidays and University closure periods. There may also be opportunities for overtime, with flexibility required to respond to changing operational needs. Further information is available in the job description . ABOUT THE TEAM Within the Campus Services team, we deliver a range of services. Please see further information about the University's Security Service here and the wider Campus Services team here . ABOUT YOU The successful candidate will demonstrate excellent communication and interpersonal skills, a positive and proactive approach, and the ability to remain calm and professional when dealing with challenging situations. You will be expected to work effectively as part of a team while also using your initiative and sound judgement when responding to incidents and supporting the wider University community. This is an excellent opportunity for individuals who are passionate about delivering high-quality customer service, promoting safety and security, and making a positive contribution to the student and staff experience. To be successful in the role, you will have: Valid SIA Door Supervisor License Full UK driving licence (Manual or Auto accepted) Experience dealing with First Aid (First Aid at Work Certificate desirable) Be part of a Fire Response Team Excellent customer service skills Ability to provide and compile concise reports Further information is available in the person specification . If you would like an informal discussion about the role, please contact Luke Anderson - luke.anderson@northumbria.ac.uk To apply for this vacancy, please click 'Apply Now'. Your application should include a covering letter and a CV. ABOUT US Join Northumbria University, a research-intensive institution unlocking potential and changing lives locally and globally. Named Times Higher Education's University of the Year in 2022 and Modern University of the Year in 2025, we rank top 25 in the UK for research power. Discover more about us . With over 37,000 students from 140+ countries, we offer world-leading research, award-winning partnerships, and an outstanding student experience. We empower our exceptional staff, promoting a positive work-life balance and offering great benefits, including excellent pension schemes, flexible working, generous holiday entitlement and more . Our Northumbria Values, co-created by our team, define who we are: Academic Excellence, Innovation, Inclusivity, Collaboration, and Ambition. Our Behaviours shape our work culture: We listen and learn, support one another, respect everyone, trust each other, and are bold. Based in Newcastle upon Tyne and London, we are an on-campus organisation and offer flexible hours and location where the role allows. We pride ourselves on diversity and inclusivity, holding numerous awards for gender and race equality, disability confidence, and research excellence. We also hold the HR Excellence in Research award for implementing the concordat supporting the career Development of Researchers and are members of the Euraxess initiative to deliver information and support to professional researchers. The University has implemented a range of flexible working arrangements, and we are happy to explore candidate requirements as part of the recruitment process. Apply now and join a community committed to transforming society and the economy. Explore \" Our Exceptional People , Our Values and Behaviours \" page to learn more about our commitments and see our Privacy Policy for further details. "
    },
    "https://fa-etnb-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/4248": {
      "title": "Senior Faculty Technician Faculty Operations Science and Environment",
      "description": "Closing Date: 22/10/2026 Category: Technical and Specialist PrimaryLocation: Newcastle City Campus, United Kingdom Applications are invited for a Senior Technician role within the Faculty of Science and Environment Technical Support Team. This role will provide specialist IT support and development across the faculty as well as specific teaching support and delivery within the School of Computer Science. ABOUT THE ROLE The post holder will be responsible for the procurement, preparation, maintenance, asset management, support and development of computing equipment, software and peripherals that support taught modules and research projects. They will also deliver demonstrations and training sessions to staff and student groups with information of a highly specialist nature. Teaching support for Computer Science has focus on the following key subject areas: Programming and Software Development Networks and Network Security Cyber Security and Digital Forensics Machine Learning\\Deep Learning\\Artificial Intelligence Cloud Computing\\Virtualisation\\Containerisation Games Development Big Data and Information Sciences As this is a senior role, candidates for the post should have and be able to display experience and in-depth knowledge of one or more of the subject areas above. Further information is available in the job description . ABOUT THE TEAM Based at the Newcastle City Campus, the Faculty IT & Research Support Team (FIRST) is a dedicated group of Research Technical Professionals, Research Software Engineers and IT Professionals that design, develop, implement and support specialist IT solutions for teaching and research across the Faculty of Science & Environment. ABOUT YOU To be successful in the role you will be proactive and enthusiastic with excellent team working and communication skills. Some experience of customer service is required and an interest in technology both hardware and software is essential. We are particularly interested in someone who as full stack development experience and likes to apply their knowledge and skills to provide novel or bespoke solutions. Further information is available in the person specification . To apply for this vacancy, please click 'Apply Now' . Your application should include a covering letter and a CV. ABOUT US Join Northumbria University, a research-intensive institution unlocking potential and changing lives locally and globally. Named Times Higher Education's University of the Year in 2022 and Modern University of the Year in 2025, we rank top 25 in the UK for research power. Discover more about us . With over 37,000 students from 140+ countries, we offer world-leading research, award-winning partnerships, and an outstanding student experience. We empower our exceptional staff, promoting a positive work-life balance and offering great benefits, including excellent pension schemes, flexible working, generous holiday entitlement and more . Our Northumbria Values, co-created by our team, define who we are: Academic Excellence, Innovation, Inclusivity, Collaboration, and Ambition. Our Behaviours shape our work culture: We listen and learn, support one another, respect everyone, trust each other, and are bold. Based in Newcastle upon Tyne and London, we are an on-campus organisation and offer flexible hours and location where the role allows. We pride ourselves on diversity and inclusivity, holding a Silver Athena Swan in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality, a bronze Race Equality Charter Award and we are a Disability Confident Leader. We also hold the HR Excellence in Research award for implementing the concordat supporting the career Development of Researchers and are members of the Euraxess initiative to deliver information and support to professional researchers. The University has implemented a range of flexible working arrangements, and we are happy to explore candidate requirements as part of the recruitment process. Apply now and join a community committed to transforming society and the economy. Explore \" Our Exceptional People , Our Values and Behaviours \" page to learn more about our commitments and see our Privacy Policy for further details. "
    },
    "https://fa-etnb-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/4271": {
      "title": "Senior Market Insight Analyst",
      "description": "Closing Date: 21/10/2026 Category: Management, Professional and Administrative PrimaryLocation: United Kingdom Join our Marketing team as a Senior Market Insight Analyst, providing expert market and competitor insight to shape Northumbria's portfolio, pricing and recruitment strategy in a fast-evolving HE sector. ABOUT THE ROLE The role holder will provide insight and intelligence to support Marketing and other internal stakeholders across the University. The role is designed to help the University strategically position product portfolio and pricing within a competitive sector landscape. This role will also lead and coordinate the development of market and customer insights, identifying key opportunities across segmented audience groups and informing campaigns and interventions. The role will support the University in understanding its positioning relative to competitors and ensure we are prepared to leverage significant shifts in market dynamics, maintaining a strong competitive advantage in an evolving environment. Further information is available in the job description ABOUT THE TEAM You will be based within UK Marketing and Student Recruitment, working as part of a collaborative and fast-paced team that plays a central role in shaping Northumbria's positioning and growth in a competitive higher education sector. The team works closely with colleagues across Marketing, as well as with academic and professional services colleagues across the University's Faculties, to ensure decisions are grounded in robust insight and evidence. Within Marketing we deliver a range of services including brand and campaign development, digital marketing, market and competitor insight, student recruitment and admissions, events and open days, and content and communications. ABOUT YOU To be successful in the role you will have: Experience of market research or analysis – ideally gained within a market research or insight function, with a track record of planning and delivering research, insight or analysis projects. Strong data analysis skills – including experience analysing large datasets and creating dashboards or other engaging ways of presenting data, with proficiency in Excel (PowerBI experience desirable). Experience of quantitative research techniques, such as questionnaire design and survey analysis. Excellent communication skills – confident delivering engaging presentations as well as writing clear, compelling reports and analysis tailored to different audiences. A good understanding of the higher education sector, including awareness of the key trends, challenges and opportunities affecting the sector, along with knowledge of marketing, insight and research principles. Strong planning and stakeholder management skills – able to manage competing priorities and deadlines while building effective working relationships across the University. Further information is available in the person specification If you would like an informal discussion about the role, please contact Laura Jobling (UK Student Recruitment and Access Manager) l.jobling@northumbria.ac.uk To apply for this vacancy please click 'Apply Now'. Your application should include a covering letter and a CV. ABOUT US Join Northumbria University, a research-intensive institution unlocking potential and changing lives locally and globally. Named Times Higher Education's University of the Year in 2022 and Modern University of the Year in 2025, we rank top 25 in the UK for research power. Discover more about us . With over 37,000 students from 140+ countries, we offer world-leading research, award-winning partnerships, and an outstanding student experience. We empower our exceptional staff, promoting a positive work-life balance and offering great benefits, including excellent pension schemes, flexible working, generous holiday entitlement and more . Our Northumbria Values, co-created by our team, define who we are: Academic Excellence, Innovation, Inclusivity, Collaboration, and Ambition. Our Behaviours shape our work culture: We listen and learn, support one another, respect everyone, trust each other, and are bold. Based in Newcastle upon Tyne and London, we are an on-campus organisation and offer flexible hours and location where the role allows. We pride ourselves on diversity and inclusivity, holding a Silver Athena Swan in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality, a bronze Race Equality Charter Award and we are a Disability Confident Leader. We also hold the HR Excellence in Research award for implementing the concordat supporting the career Development of Researchers and are members of the Euraxess initiative to deliver information and support to professional researchers. The University has implemented a range of flexible working arrangements, and we are happy to explore candidate requirements as part of the recruitment process. Apply now and join a community committed to transforming society and the economy. Explore \" Our Exceptional People , Our Values and Behaviours \" page to learn more about our commitments and see our Privacy Policy for further details. "
    },
    "https://fa-etnb-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/4249": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Closing Date: 25/10/2026 Category: Teaching and Research PrimaryLocation: Newcastle City Campus, United Kingdom Working as part of multi-disciplinary teams, you will support the Principal Investigator in delivery of research activity on the PELLIS: Investigating the manufacture, trade, and economy of Roman leather through multi-analytical approaches. ABOUT THE ROLE Working as part of multi-disciplinary teams, you will support the Principal Investigator in delivery of research activity on the PELLIS: Investigating the manufacture, trade, and economy of Roman leather through multi-analytical approaches. The AHRC-funded PELLIS project investigates the manufacture, trade, and economic role of leather in the Roman Empire, addressing a major gap in our understanding of an industry essential to both civilian and military life. Because archaeological leather survives only in exceptional conditions, its economic significance has long been overshadowed by more durable materials like pottery. Through an interdisciplinary approach combining proteomics, stable isotope and genetic analysis with archaeological and historical research, PELLIS will analyse leather assemblages from Roman Britain, the Netherlands, and Syria to determine animal species, origins, processing methods, and patterns of object production and use. By integrating this new scientific evidence with broader economic models, the project will reveal how leather was produced, distributed, and accessed across the provinces and frontiers, offering a more holistic picture of Roman manufacturing, supply networks, husbandry practices, and everyday lifeways. The post is funded for 32 months and is available on a full-time basis (1.0 FTE). ABOUT THE TEAM This Research Project is being delivered within the school of Geography and Natural sciences, you will join Professor Gillian taylor research group funded by a AHRC research grant. The team includes colleagues at collaborating Institutes (University College London, North Carolina State University, and University of Western Ontario) for the scientific analysis of Roman leather and the interpretation of finding in the wider context of the Roman ecology. The primary role of the post holder will be: to assist with the collection of samples for biomolecular analyses; use of analytical instrumentation including spectroscopy for degradation analysis; prepare samples for biomolecular analysis of leather including ZooMS (Zooarchaeology by Mass Spectrometry); data interpretation and integration with wider Roman archaeological evidence, writing/contributing to publications, assisting with project outreach. ABOUT YOU Applicants should hold a PhD (or be close to completion) in bioarchaeology/molecular/analytical chemistry or related discipline. We welcome applicants from a range of backgrounds, and who are keen to apply their skills to archaeological research. Further information about the requirements of the role is available in the person specification . If you would like an informal discussion about the role, please contact gillian.taylor@northumbria.ac.uk To apply for this vacancy please click 'Apply Now' . Your application should include a covering letter and a CV. ABOUT US Join Northumbria University, a research-intensive institution unlocking potential and changing lives locally and globally. Named Times Higher Education's University of the Year in 2022 and Modern University of the Year in 2025, we rank top 25 in the UK for research power. Discover more about us . With over 37,000 students from 140+ countries, we offer world-leading research, award-winning partnerships, and an outstanding student experience. We empower our exceptional staff, promoting a positive work-life balance and offering great benefits, including excellent pension schemes, flexible working, generous holiday entitlement and more . Our Northumbria Values, co-created by our team, define who we are: Academic Excellence, Innovation, Inclusivity, Collaboration, and Ambition. Our Behaviours shape our work culture: We listen and learn, support one another, respect everyone, trust each other, and are bold. Based in Newcastle upon Tyne and London, we are an on-campus organisation and offer flexible hours and location where the role allows. We pride ourselves on diversity and inclusivity, holding a Silver Athena Swan in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality, a bronze Race Equality Charter Award and we are a Disability Confident Leader. We also hold the HR Excellence in Research award for implementing the concordat supporting the career Development of Researchers and are members of the Euraxess initiative to deliver information and support to professional researchers. The University has implemented a range of flexible working arrangements, and we are happy to explore candidate requirements as part of the recruitment process. Apply now and join a community committed to transforming society and the economy. Explore \" Our Exceptional People , Our Values and Behaviours \" page to learn more about our commitments and see our Privacy Policy for further details. "
    },
    "https://fa-etnb-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/4275": {
      "title": "Faculty Technician - Engineering, Architecture and Built Environment",
      "description": "Closing Date: 14/10/2026 Category: Technical and Specialist PrimaryLocation: Newcastle City Campus, United Kingdom We are looking for a front-facing engineering technician with excellent communication and organisational skills to join our technical team. ABOUT THE ROLE We are recruiting a full time technician post based in the Faculty of Science and Environment supporting the the School of Engineering, Physics and Mathematics and the School of Architecture and Built Environment . The role holder will work with stakeholders across these areas, but there will be a focus on support towards the Civil Engineering and the Built Environment departments. The role holder will support stakeholders ranging from undergraduate students to research staff. This will be by supporting the Senior Technicians in maintaining laboratories and workshops to a high standard, ensuring they are well stocked and safe for users for practical taught sessions and research projects. A large part of this role is to work within an equipment store, using our inventory software to distribute equipment to staff and students to support their work and ensuring it is checked on return. The role holder will have the opportunity to work across all areas the technical team support which include laboratories and workshops with metal working, wood working, 3D printing, electronics many more. ABOUT THE TEAM Working across both the School of Engineering, Physics and Mathematics and the School of Architecture and Built Environment, both in the Faculty of Science and Environmental, the technical team offer support to a wide range of studios, laboratories and workshops providing excellent learning and research environments to all of our stakeholders. The successful candidate will based in Ellison Building, City Centre Campus and will work alongside other Technicians and Senior Technicians covering the disciplines offered by the schools supported. ABOUT YOU The successful candidate will be qualified to level 2 and have experience in a discipline related to the role. Experience of working in a university and customer service experience would be beneficial, as would work in the field of civil engineering. You will possess excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to guide, train and support a diverse range of users, from students and early-career researchers to experienced academics and external partners. You will be equally comfortable working independently and contributing as part of a collaborative technical team. Strong organisational skills and a willing attitude are essential in the role. Further information is available in the person specification . If you would like an informal discussion about the role, please contact Sam Hutchinson To apply for this vacancy please click 'Apply Now'. Your application should include a covering letter and a CV. ABOUT US Join Northumbria University, a research-intensive institution unlocking potential and changing lives locally and globally. Named Times Higher Education's University of the Year in 2022 and Modern University of the Year in 2025, we rank top 25 in the UK for research power. Discover more about us . With over 37,000 students from 140+ countries, we offer world-leading research, award-winning partnerships, and an outstanding student experience. We empower our exceptional staff, promoting a positive work-life balance and offering great benefits, including excellent pension schemes, flexible working, generous holiday entitlement and more . Our Northumbria Values, co-created by our team, define who we are: Academic Excellence, Innovation, Inclusivity, Collaboration, and Ambition. Our Behaviours shape our work culture: We listen and learn, support one another, respect everyone, trust each other, and are bold. Based in Newcastle upon Tyne and London, we are an on-campus organisation and offer flexible hours and location where the role allows. We pride ourselves on diversity and inclusivity, holding a Silver Athena Swan in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality, a bronze Race Equality Charter Award and we are a Disability Confident Leader. We also hold the HR Excellence in Research award for implementing the concordat supporting the career Development of Researchers and are members of the Euraxess initiative to deliver information and support to professional researchers. The University has implemented a range of flexible working arrangements, and we are happy to explore candidate requirements as part of the recruitment process. Apply now and join a community committed to transforming society and the economy. Explore \" Our Exceptional People , Our Values and Behaviours \" page to learn more about our commitments and see our Privacy Policy for further details. "
    },
    "https://fa-etnb-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/4035": {
      "title": "KTP Associate: Software and Analytics Specialist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 13/10/2026 Category: Teaching and Research PrimaryLocation: External, United Kingdom To be part of an exciting collaboration between Northumbria University and Identity Consult, we are looking for a self-motivated and innovative Software Engineering Associate who wants to help put sustainability and the environment at the heart of the built environment. To be part of an exciting collaboration between Northumbria University and Identity Consult, we are looking for a self-motivated and innovative Software Engineering Associate who wants to help put sustainability and the environment at the heart of the built environment. This is a genuine opportunity to use your technical skills to transform how a whole industry designs, builds and decarbonises, working at the intersection of academia and industry to deliver real, measurable impact. The ideal candidate will hold a degree in software engineering or design (preferably at postgraduate level) and bring strong software and database development skills, combined with the ability to manage their own work and deliver high-quality outcomes to deadlines. You will be curious, adaptable and enthusiastic about technology, with a genuine interest in applying your expertise to one of the built environment's biggest challenges: reducing its environmental impact. Experience with agile development, UX/UI design, data structures, and data visualisation would be highly beneficial, alongside an ability to collaborate effectively within multidisciplinary teams and communicate complex ideas through clear reports and publications. We are particularly keen to hear from candidates who enjoy problem-solving, learning new tools and concepts quickly, and building productive relationships between academic researchers, industry partners, and wider stakeholders to deliver innovation that genuinely matters. You will be delivering an ambitious innovation project at the leading edge of built environment decarbonisation: developing a sector-leading multi-modal platform that integrates proprietary carbon and cost data into a centralised, client-facing system. This is a rare chance to build technology that puts carbon reduction directly into everyday design and investment decisions, helping reshape how an entire industry thinks about sustainability. The project will call on your expertise in software development, database architecture, carbon-cost modelling, and user design, with critical supporting capabilities in data governance, cybersecurity, and AI-driven analytics for managing proprietary datasets and extracting new value. You will be joining Identity Consult, an award-winning, independent development and construction consultancy genuinely committed to delivering sustainable innovation across the built environment, working with them at their base in Quay West Business Village in Sunderland. You will lead a transformation project, a Knowledge Transfer Partnership (KTP), placing sustainability and environmental impact at the very heart of your work. Based at Identity Consult, you will also work closely with an academic team from Northumbria University, giving you direct access to leading research expertise in sustainability, carbon modelling and the built environment. The opportunities available to you: Leading and delivering a challenging and strategic R&D project on decarbonisation of the Built Environment that directly impacts the business and their clients Support from an academic team with specialist knowledge and experience relevant to the project Potential to be retained in a permanent position within the company at the end of the project A professional training and personal development budget totalling £2,000 per annum. For full details of qualifications, skills and experience required for the post, please review the role description . Please note that this job is located at the company premises in Quay West Business Village in Sunderland, a city undergoing a genuinely exciting transformation of its own. Sunderland is currently the focus of an ambitious £2 billion investment, development and urban regeneration programme. Riverside Sunderland's ten-year growth plan, adjacent to Quay West Business Village, is now well underway, boosting the business, tourist and cultural economies while building attractive and sustainable homes and communities for generations to come. More information can be found here: https://riversidesunderland.com/ Find out more about Identity Consult and their commitment to sustainable innovation at the following link: https://www.identityconsult.co.uk/ Find out more about the School of Architecture and Built Environment at Northumbria University, where sustainability and real-world impact are at the heart of everything we do, at the following link: https://www.northumbria.ac.uk/about-us/our-schools/school-of-architecture-and-built-environment/ To arrange an informal phone conversation with one of the project team, please contact: Dr Kevin Muldoon-Smith: k.muldoon-smith@northumbria.ac.uk This role is not eligible under the Global Talent Visa (Endorsed Funder route). Applicants requiring Skilled Worker visa sponsorship will be considered on a case-by-case basis, subject to individual circumstances and UK immigration requirements. To apply for this vacancy please click 'Apply Now', and submit a covering letter, CV including research/education statements, and publications list. Contract Duration: Fixed term for 18 months Start date: estimating December 2026 (to be agreed) Interview date: The interview will take place approximately 1-2 weeks after the application closing date. ABOUT NORTHUMBRIA UNIVERSITY Join Northumbria University, a research-intensive institution unlocking potential and changing lives locally and globally. Named Times Higher Education's University of the Year in 2022 and Modern University of the Year in 2025, we rank top 25 in the UK for research power. Discover more about us . With over 37,000 students from 140+ countries, we offer world-leading research, award-winning partnerships, and an outstanding student experience. We empower our exceptional staff, promoting a positive work-life balance and offering great benefits, including excellent pension schemes, flexible working, generous holiday entitlement and more . Our Northumbria Values, co-created by our team, define who we are: Academic Excellence, Innovation, Inclusivity, Collaboration, and Ambition. Our Behaviours shape our work culture: We listen and learn, support one another, respect everyone, trust each other, and are bold. Based in Newcastle upon Tyne and London, we are an on-campus organisation and offer flexible hours and location where the role allows. We pride ourselves on diversity and inclusivity, holding numerous awards for gender and race equality, disability confidence, and research excellence. We also hold the HR Excellence in Research award for implementing the concordat supporting the career Development of Researchers and are members of the Euraxess initiative to deliver information and support to professional researchers. The University has implemented a range of flexible working arrangements, and we are happy to explore candidate requirements as part of the recruitment process. Apply now and join a community committed to transforming society and the economy. Explore \" Our Exceptional People , Our Values and Behaviours \" page to learn more about our commitments and see our Privacy Policy for further details. "
    },
    "https://fa-etnb-saasfaprod1.fa.ocs.oraclecloud.com/hcmUI/CandidateExperience/en/sites/CX_1001/job/4243": {
      "title": "Pre-Award Development Officer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 14/10/2026 Category: Management, Professional and Administrative PrimaryLocation: Newcastle City Campus, United Kingdom We are looking for a Pre-Award Development Officer who will provide high-quality professional and expert support to researchers across a wide range of disciplines. ABOUT THE ROLE Based in the Grants and Contracts Team (Pre-Award) within Research and Innovation Services, the Pre-Award Development Officer will provide high-quality professional and expert support to researchers across a wide range of disciplines; preparing project costings for research grant applications; providing expert advice and guidance to academic staff on funding streams, and particular requirements of funders; and ensuring that all applications receive authorisation in accordance with the University’s procedures. Reporting to the Pre-Award Team Manager, and working as part of a team, the successful candidate will support researchers to develop and secure external research grants via the provision of specialist support at every stage of the pre-award process. The post-holder will also contribute to the planning, support and implementation of Research Development related communications activity including publicising key opportunities and initiatives. The successful candidate will have strong organisational, planning, analytical, problem-solving and communication skills, along with the ability to prioritise competing demands and make independent decisions in a fast-paced environment. They will build and maintain effective working relationships, demonstrate sound judgement in guiding others, and bring a strong understanding of the UK funding landscape and knowledge of research funding bodies. Further information is available in the job description . ABOUT THE TEAM For more information about RIS and the support we provide to Northumbria's research community, please visit our webpage: https://www.northumbria.ac.uk/about-us/university-services/research-and-innovation-services/ Northumbria University is a vibrant, campus-based institution and the successful candidate will be expected to spend the majority of their time on campus at our Newcastle City location. This is a people-facing managerial role where presence matters, both for the effective management of teams and for building the strong internal relationships that are central to success in this post. We recognise the importance of work-life balance and have flexible and hybrid working arrangements in place, which will be agreed in line with the needs of the role and the service. ABOUT YOU Experienced in costing and preparing externally funded research proposals, with sound knowledge of fEC principles and costing methodology A strong understanding of the UK research funding landscape and national funder requirements Skilled at managing multiple, competing priorities and tight deadlines without compromising accuracy Confident building relationships and influencing stakeholders at all levels, from researchers to funders Able to interpret complex written documentation and constructively review academic text A proactive problem-solver, comfortable working independently under broad direction Experience of Higher Education (or a similarly complex research environment) and, ideally, line management Further information is available in the person specification . If you would like an informal discussion about the role, please contact Vicky Boon, Pre-Award Team Manager (victoria.boon@northumbria.ac.uk) To apply for this vacancy please click 'Apply Now'. Your application should include a covering letter and a CV. ABOUT US Join Northumbria University, a research-intensive institution unlocking potential and changing lives locally and globally. Named Times Higher Education's University of the Year in 2022 and Modern University of the Year in 2025, we rank top 25 in the UK for research power. Discover more about us . With over 37,000 students from 140+ countries, we offer world-leading research, award-winning partnerships, and an outstanding student experience. We empower our exceptional staff, promoting a positive work-life balance and offering great benefits, including excellent pension schemes, flexible working, generous holiday entitlement and more . Our Northumbria Values, co-created by our team, define who we are: Academic Excellence, Innovation, Inclusivity, Collaboration, and Ambition. Our Behaviours shape our work culture: We listen and learn, support one another, respect everyone, trust each other, and are bold. Based in Newcastle upon Tyne and London, we are an on-campus organisation and offer flexible hours and location where the role allows. We pride ourselves on diversity and inclusivity, holding a Silver Athena Swan in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality, a bronze Race Equality Charter Award and we are a Disability Confident Leader. We also hold the HR Excellence in Research award for implementing the concordat supporting the career Development of Researchers and are members of the Euraxess initiative to deliver information and support to professional researchers. The University has implemented a range of flexible working arrangements, and we are happy to explore candidate requirements as part of the recruitment process. Apply now and join a community committed to transforming society and the economy. Explore \" Our Exceptional People , Our Values and Behaviours \" page to learn more about our commitments and see our Privacy Policy for further details. "
    }
